  Actually the interface is badly broken.  Critical information is
placed at the top of the screen, the lower right of the screen, and
buried in the lower left of the screen.  Not to mention the need to
watch your character closely, which can be almost anywhere else.

  It's not just the need to jump your eyes around a lot.  You also have
to focus and read the data.  Not like dials or gauges where relative
position is enough.  That's why the hpmon patch is so important.  Color
is a lot easier to determine than reading two numbers and calculating a
percentage after every move.  Menu colors can be almost as important.
Sortloot also helps a lot.  Properly prepared autopickup exceptions
ditto.

  In NetHack you spend way too much mental effort fighting the interface
that you desperately need to play the game instead.

  After extended periods of careful play I get eyestrain.  Really.

  This is terrible interface design.  Think of the Heads Up Display
(HUD) in jet fighters.  Better yet, there's the story of a bush pilot in
Alaska who carefully dismounted every dial in his cockpit.  The
passenger was worried until takeoff.  Every gauge had been adjusted so
that in normal operation the needle was straight up.  Instrument scan
was a breeze.

  Just moving the messages from the top of the screen to the bottom
would be a big help.  So would moving hitpoints to the right of the turn
count.


  That's a big part of what makes NetHack so difficult.  Lack of
keyboard macros for complicated actions.  Try switching from
#twoweaponing to a pickaxe and then back again.  It's an incredible
pain, and for no apparent reason other than to punish the player for
playing NetHack.



